TRANSP:OPAQUE
SUMMARY:Are international NGOs helping (or hurting) the causes they set out to solve?
DESCRIPTION:International non-governmental organizations (INGOs) have emerged over the past few decades as the dominant institution in the civil society ecosystem, garnering a disproportionate amount of both resources and power in the international arena. Local actors continue to be marginalized and largely excluded from decision-making. Many often act as little more than sub-contractors to the INGO sector or face direct competition as INGOs have sought to set up their own local organizations.\nBut this dominant position is under threat. INGOs are now on the precipice of decline, having to face up to a series of challenges in recent years: from sexual exploitation scandals, to accusations of white-saviourism, resulting in ever-louder demands to decolonize and shift power and resources more directly to local actors. In some cases, funders are now heeding these demands, turning away from INGOs altogether.\nThis event explores these challenges, drawing from the personal experience Deborah Doane, author and convenor of The RINGO Project, and delves into the stories of leaders across all sectors of global civil society. It builds on the work of the RINGO Project, a systems-change endeavour to “Reimagine the INGO” and its relationships with local civil society organisations. The project explores difficult issues around race and power, and offers ideas for new systems of accountability, risk, funding, governance and more.\nThe INGO problem has sparked a global debate on the role of INGOs and their dominant role in civil society.\n \n
URL:https://events.yorku.ca/events/are-international-ngos-helping-or-hurting-the-causes-they-set-out-to-solve/
CATEGORIES:16 Days of Activism,Co-op &amp; Career Centre,Graduate Program in Development Studies,Graduate Program in Public Policy, Administration and Law,Graduate Program in Sociology,International,LA&amp;PS Colleges,LA&amp;PS Students-International,Research &amp; Innovation,SDG Month,Sustainability
LOCATION:2101 Vari Hall, Keele Campus
END:VEVENT
