SUMMARY:Artificial Intelligence and the Law: New Challenges and Possibilities for Fundamental Human Rights and Security
DESCRIPTION:Update: Due to the labour disruption, this conference will now be held online only.\nThe Jack and Mae Nathanson Centre on Transnational Human Rights, Crime and Security will hold an online conference titled Artificial Intelligence and the Law: New Challenges and Possibilities for Fundamental Human Rights and Security on March 13.\nRegister: bit.ly/47YnHyC.\nArtificial Intelligence (AI) is dramatically reshaping how people live, work and interact, as well as the functioning of societies and legal systems’ adaptations to these changes. Machine learning technologies’ integration into various decision-making processes carries profound implications for sentencing, taxation, workplace dynamics, surveillance and policing, privacy and financial markets. The rising automation of human activities prompts significant legal inquiries spanning constitutional, contractual and tort issues.\nLarge language models such as ChatGPT are AI technologies with a range of legal, ethical and societal implications. These models, trained on massive volumes of text data, can generate text resembling human language, enabling tasks like answering questions, writing essays and even crafting poetry. They implicate freedom of expression, the right to information and the democratic process at large. They have the potential to generate misleading, harmful or hateful content, regardless of their programmers’ and owners’ intentions. They could become tools for propaganda or disinformation campaigns. They raise intellectual property questions, particularly when their output is based on pre-existing intellectual or artistic works and could lead to mass job automation.\nOn March 13, we will meet virtually to discuss all these issues with a stellar group of researchers and speakers.\nAGENDA\nNoon to 12:15 p.m.\nIntroduction to the Conference\nDean Trevor Farrow ( https://www.osgoode.yorku.ca/faculty-and-staff/farrow-trevor-c-w/ )\nBarnali Choudhury ( https://www.osgoode.yorku.ca/faculty-and-staff/choudhury-barnali/ )\nValerio De Stefano ( https://www.osgoode.yorku.ca/faculty-and-staff/de-stefano-valerio/ )\n12:15 p.m. to 2:15 p.m.\nArtificial Intelligence and the Law – Barnali Choudhury, Chair\nAllan Hutchinson ( https://www.osgoode.yorku.ca/faculty-and-staff/hutchinson-allan-c/ ): Reflections on Singularity: AI and Law’s Multiplicity.\nJon Penney ( https://www.osgoode.yorku.ca/faculty-and-staff/penney-jonathon/ ): How Safe Are AI Safety Standards?\nCarys Craig ( https://www.osgoode.yorku.ca/faculty-and-staff/craig-carys-j/ ): The AI-Copyright Trap.\nValerio De Stefano ( https://www.osgoode.yorku.ca/faculty-and-staff/de-stefano-valerio/ ): Artificial Intelligence and Work.\nAida Abraha ( https://www.linkedin.com/in/aida-p-a-6337827/?original_referer= ): Examining AI Governance in the Workplace Context: A Comparative Analysis of Workplace Technology Regulations in Canada, the United States, and the European Union.\nFrançois Tanguay-Renaud ( https://www.osgoode.yorku.ca/faculty-and-staff/tanguay-renaud-francois/ ): Contrasting Police Powers of Detention and Arrest in Canada and the United States: Is There a Place for Predictive AI and Some Thoughts about Racial Profiling and its Regulation.\n2:15 p.m. to 2:30 p.m.\nCoffee break\n2:30 p.m. to 4:30 p.m.\nArtificial intelligence and Human Rights Protection – Dean Trevor Farrow, Chair \nSean Rehaag ( https://www.osgoode.yorku.ca/faculty-and-staff/rehaag-sean/ ): Rights-Enhancing Tech: Using AI to Open the Black Box of Human Refugee Adjudication.\nJake Okechukwu Effoduh ( https://ng.linkedin.com/in/effoduh ): How Artificial Intelligence is Bastardizing Paradigms of Human Rights in the Third World.\nJames Sheptycki ( https://profiles.laps.yorku.ca/profiles/jshep/ ): AI and the police intelligence division-of-labour; a Canadian perspective.\nAlexandra Scott ( https://ca.linkedin.com/in/alexandra-scott-6985bb196 ): Autonomous weapons systems and International Humanitarian Law.\nAnthony Sangiuliano ( https://anthonysangiuliano.com/home ): Approaches to Prohibiting Algorithmic Discrimination under the Canadian Human Rights Act.\nLuna Xiaolu Li: Regulating Algorithmic Discrimination with Affirmative Action: A Comparative and Interdisciplinary Study.\nAneurin Thomas ( https://ca.linkedin.com/in/nye-thomas-58460511b ): Regulating Police Facial Recognition Technology:  Issues and Options.\n4:30 p.m. to 4:45 p.m.\nCoffee break\n4:45 p.m. to 6:15 p.m.\nArtificial Intelligence, Due Process and Legal Ethics – Roundtable – Valerio De Stefano, Chair\n\nDean Trevor Farrow ( https://www.osgoode.yorku.ca/faculty-and-staff/farrow-trevor-c-w/ ), Osgoode Hall Law School\nGlenn Stuart ( https://ca.linkedin.com/in/glenn-stuart-612b198 ), Law Society of Ontario\nAmy Salyzyn ( https://www.uottawa.ca/faculty-law/common-law/faculty/salyzyn-amy-f ), University of Ottawa\nPatricia McMahon ( https://www.osgoode.yorku.ca/faculty-and-staff/patricia-mcmahon/ ), Osgoode Hall Law School\nRichard Haigh ( https://www.osgoode.yorku.ca/faculty-and-staff/haigh-richard/ ) and Stephen Fulford ( https://ca.linkedin.com/in/stephen-fulford-496955114 ), Osgoode Hall Law School\nGiuseppina (Pina) D’Agostino ( https://www.osgoode.yorku.ca/faculty-and-staff/dagostino-giuseppina/ ), Osgoode Hall Law School\nThe event will be livestreaming online. For any further information, feel free to contact Professor Valerio De Stefano at nathansoncentre@osgoode.yorku.ca ( mailto:nathansoncentre@osgoode.yorku.ca ) and copy vdestefano@osgoode.yorku.ca ( mailto:vdestefano@osgoode.yorku.ca ).\n
URL:https://events.yorku.ca/events/artificial-intelligence-and-the-law-new-challenges-and-possibilities-for-fundamental-human-rights-and-security/
CATEGORIES:Osgoode Hall Law School
LOCATION:(URL will be provided in the email confirmation)
