LAST-MODIFIED:20240129
PRIORITY:5
SEQUENCE:2
TRANSP:OPAQUE
SUMMARY:What’s Happening in Ecuador: The Origins and Dimensions of Violence
DESCRIPTION:What are the underlying structural causes of the drug-trafficking violence that engulfed parts of Ecuador in early January? In which regions of Ecuador was/is the violence and the drug trade concentrated and why?\nWhat are the global/external factors that promote the continued growth of narco-trafficking? Does the government’s militarized strategy to combat gangs involved in narco-trafficking make sense?\nHave such repressive policies worked elsewhere in Latin America or anywhere else? What are the alternative policies that might be pursued?\nChaired by Patrick Taylor and Liisa North, CERLAC fellows from York University.\nCarlos Larrea is a graduate in philosophy, Catholic University of Ecuador, Quito; master in social sciences, Bariloche Foundation; PhD in political thought, York University, Toronto; postdoctoral in health and development, Harvard University and Pan American Health Organization, Boston. He is an author of numerous books and articles on political-economic and environmental issues.\nThis event is being co-sponsored by Canadian Association of Latin American and Caribbean Studies (CALACS) and by the Department of Spanish & Portuguese at the University of Toronto.\n
URL:https://events.yorku.ca/events/whats-happening-in-ecuador-the-origins-and-dimensions-of-violence/
ORGANIZER;CN=CERLAC:MAILTO:cerlac@yorku.ca
CATEGORIES:Faculty of Liberal Arts &amp; Professional Studies,Graduate Program in Development Studies,Graduate Program in Economics,Graduate Program in Gender, Feminist and Women's Studies,Graduate Program in Geography,Graduate Program in Global Health,Graduate Program in History,Graduate Program in Humanities,Graduate Program in Law,Graduate Program in Linguistics &amp; Applied Linguistics,Graduate Program in Public Policy, Administration and Law,Graduate Program in Social &amp; Political Thought,Graduate Program in Social Anthrolology,Graduate Program in Socio-Legal Studies,LA&amp;PS Department of Anthropology,LA&amp;PS Department of Equity Studies,LA&amp;PS Department of History,LA&amp;PS Department of Humanities,LA&amp;PS Department of Languages, Literatures &amp; Linguistics,LA&amp;PS Department of Politics,LA&amp;PS Department of Social Science,LA&amp;PS Department of Sociology
END:VEVENT
