SUMMARY:‘Writing Home’ Public Lecture featuring Bonnie Devine
DESCRIPTION:Responding to questions about how to begin a conversation with the land, Devine will present a personal journey of walking, listening, looking and making. Through this work, Devine invites viewers to join her on the journey and encourages them to think about their own relationship with the land and the importance of acknowledging Indigenous histories and perspectives.\nBonnie Devine is a prominent Anishinaabe artist, painter, curator, writer and educator who lives and works in Toronto, Ontario. She is a multidisciplinary artist whose works include sculpture, painting, video, performances, drawing and site-specific interventions. Her work is influenced by storytelling and narratives of treaty, land, environment and history of the Anishinaabeg. Bonnie is an Emerita Associate Professor at the Ontario College of Art and Design University, founding Chair of the school’s Indigenous Visual Cultural Program and recipient of a Governor General’s Award in Visual and Media Arts in 2021.\nAll York community members are invited to attend this in-person event as part of National Indigenous History Month. Please RSVP ( https://president.apps01.yorku.ca/forms/view.php?id=261411 ) to secure your spot.\n
URL:https://events.yorku.ca/events/york-university-national-indigenous-history-month-event-writing-home-guest-lecture-featuring-bonnie-devine/
CATEGORIES:Equity, Diversity &amp; Inclusion,National Indigenous History Month,Special Themes
LOCATION:Helliwell Centre
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://events.yorku.ca/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/National-Indigenous-History-Month_York-Events-Calendar-image.png
END:VEVENT
