SUMMARY:Omiwatari: Documentary Screening and Dialogue on the Cultural Losses of Climate Change
DESCRIPTION:Omiwatari depicts the first year in memory that Lake Suwa, site of a millennia-old phenomenon, remained ice-free all winter. The post-industrial landscapes of Japan’s rust belt bear witness to the price we pay for progress and our remaining possibilities to adapt to those losses. Within the next 50 years, the gods of this lake, whose annual reunion across the ice brought good fortune and balance between humans and nature, will never meet again. We are the cause, and it is up to us to find a way forward for ourselves.\n \nFeaturing:\nSapna Sharma, Department of Biology, York University\nZeesy Powers, filmmaker\n \nOmiwatari is based on the research of Dr. Sapna Sharma and in collaboration with Mr. Kiyoshi Miyasaka, chief priest of the Tenaga Jinga shrine. It is presented as part of the 2023 World Water Day Conference.\n \nA dialogue on the cultural losses of climate change and a Q&A will follow the screening.\n \nPresented with the support of the Sharma Lab, Department of Biology, Lassonde School of Engineering and the York Centre for Asian Research.\n
URL:https://events.yorku.ca/events/omiwatari-documentary-screening-and-dialogue-on-the-cultural-losses-of-climate-change/
CATEGORIES:Earth Month,Faculty of Environmental &amp; Urban Change,Faculty of Science,Glendon Biology,Glendon International Studies,Graduate Program in Communication and Culture,Graduate Program in Development Studies,Graduate Program in Digital Media,Graduate Program in Film,Graduate Program in Geography,Graduate Program in History,International,LA&amp;PS Students All,Lassonde School of Engineering,Research &amp; Innovation
LOCATION:Second Floor, Second Student Centre
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://events.yorku.ca/wp-content/uploads/2023/02/omiwatari-screening-20032023-scaled-e1677074424158.jpg
END:VEVENT
